{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>If his master gives him a wife</b> — the verse speaks of a Canaanite woman. Or is it not rather an Israelite woman? Scripture states: <b>the woman and her children shall be her master's</b> — so the verse speaks of a Canaanite woman. This is the language of the second baraita cited in the Mekhilta. Rashi writes (Rashi on Exodus 21:4) that the Hebrew woman also goes free at six years, and even before six if she developed signs, as it is stated (Deuteronomy 15:12), \"If your brother, the Hebrew, is sold to you, or the Hebrew woman.\" But this is not precisely correct, for if we say she is an Israelite woman, this is not about one who sells his daughter as a maidservant, who goes free upon developing signs — for how could his master give her to him when he does not have the power to hand her over to any other man? And likewise, the proof he cited that she too goes free at six is only according to his own words, which he wrote regarding one who sells himself, that he is not sold except for six years — but these are in the Talmud the words of an individual opinion (Kiddushin 14b), and the halakhah regarding one who sells himself is that he may be sold for six years and more than six years. If so, a woman does not go free at six except when her father sold her. But what they said — \"so the verse speaks of a Canaanite woman\" — is because it states <b>the woman and her children shall be her master's</b>, for the children of a Canaanite woman belong to the master, since the child of a Canaanite maidservant is like her (ibid. 66b), whereas with an Israelite woman, even if she were an adult, and even if we were to say that the woman is permitted to sell herself, her children belong to the father."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:12:45.771485317Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}